Loan Approval Prediction
Elevvo Pathways - Machine Learning Track
Level 2 | Task 4
1. Project Overview
This project applies machine learning classification techniques to predict whether a loan application will be approved or rejected.

The objectives are to:

Explore and understand the loan dataset.
Clean and preprocess the data.
Develop classification models.
Investigate class imbalance handling.
Compare model performance using appropriate evaluation metrics.
2. Dataset Description
Dataset: Loan Approval Prediction Dataset

Source: Kaggle Loan Prediction Dataset, accessed through a public GitHub CSV copy.

Total records: 614
Original columns: 13
Predictive features: 11
Target variable: Loan_Status
Target classes:

Y: Loan Approved
N: Loan Rejected
Loan_ID was excluded from model training because it is an identifier.

3. Exploratory Data Analysis
EDA activities included:

Dataset inspection.
Missing-value analysis.
Descriptive statistics.
Numerical feature distributions.
Categorical feature distributions.
Outlier inspection.
Analysis of relationships between features and loan approval.
Target distribution:

Approved: 422 (68.73%)
Rejected: 192 (31.27%)
The dataset exhibits moderate class imbalance.

4. Data Preprocessing
The following techniques were applied:

Removed Loan_ID.
Separated features and target variable.
Used an 80:20 stratified train-test split.
Imputed numerical missing values using the median.
Imputed categorical missing values using the most frequent value.
Applied StandardScaler to numerical features.
Applied One-Hot Encoding to categorical features.
Training records: 491 Testing records: 123

Preprocessing transformations were fitted on training data to prevent data leakage.

5. Machine Learning Models
Six model configurations were evaluated:

Logistic Regression
Balanced Logistic Regression
Decision Tree
Random Forest
Corrected SMOTENC Logistic Regression
Corrected SMOTENC Decision Tree
6. Handling Class Imbalance
Two approaches were investigated.

Class Weighting
The class_weight='balanced' option was used to give additional importance to the minority class.

SMOTENC
SMOTENC was used to generate synthetic minority-class observations while considering both categorical and numerical features.

Training distribution before resampling:

Approved: 337
Rejected: 154
Training distribution after resampling:

Approved: 337
Rejected: 337
The test set remained unchanged.

After SMOTENC, One-Hot Encoding was applied to categorical features before training the corrected classifiers.

7. Model Evaluation
Evaluation metrics included:

Accuracy
Macro Precision
Macro Recall
Macro F1-score
Rejected-class Recall
Classification Reports
Confusion Matrices
Final Model Comparison
Model	Accuracy	Macro Precision	Macro Recall	Macro F1
Logistic Regression	0.8618	0.8983	0.7836	0.8147
Balanced Logistic Regression	0.8293	0.8009	0.7964	0.7986
Random Forest	0.8293	0.8168	0.7673	0.7846
Decision Tree	0.7886	0.7535	0.7670	0.7592
SMOTENC Decision Tree	0.7642	0.7273	0.7421	0.7331
SMOTENC Logistic Regression	0.7561	0.7220	0.7435	0.7288
8. Key Findings
Logistic Regression achieved the highest accuracy of 86.18%.
Logistic Regression achieved the highest Macro F1-score of 0.8147.
Balanced Logistic Regression achieved the highest Macro Recall of 0.7964.
Balanced Logistic Regression and corrected SMOTENC Logistic Regression achieved the highest rejected-class recall of 0.7105.
SMOTENC did not improve overall performance compared with the original Logistic Regression.
Class weighting improved minority-class detection compared with the original Logistic Regression.
9. Best Performing Model
Logistic Regression was selected as the best overall model based on its Macro F1-score.

Performance:

Accuracy: 86.18%
Macro Precision: 89.83%
Macro Recall: 78.36%
Macro F1-score: 81.47%
Balanced Logistic Regression is a useful alternative when detecting rejected applications is a higher priority.

10. Project Limitations
The dataset contains only 614 observations.
The dataset may not represent all financial institutions or geographical regions.
Imbalance-handling techniques did not consistently improve performance.
Evaluation used one stratified train-test split.
Further validation and hyperparameter tuning are recommended.
11. Future Improvements
Apply cross-validation.
Perform additional hyperparameter optimisation.
Investigate classification threshold optimisation.
Explore additional classification algorithms.
Test on larger and more diverse datasets.
Investigate model explainability using SHAP.
Develop a loan prediction application.
